            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"Christ as the exclusive means of access to salvation, eternal life, and fellowship with God; more broadly, the transition point or entrance into the Kingdom of God.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"John 10:9\",\"Matthew 7:13-14\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"Evangelistic or missionary opportunity for the Gospel\",\"basis\":[\"1 Corinthians 16:9\",\"Colossians 4:3\",\"Revelation 3:8\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Civic authority, defensive power, or the place of judgment and governance\",\"basis\":[\"Matthew 16:18\",\"Ruth 4:1\",\"Deuteronomy 16:18\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"The human heart, personal will, or individual receptivity to God\",\"basis\":[\"Revelation 3:20\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"In biblical symbolism, doors and gates predominantly represent access points. In the New Testament, this is heavily Christological (Christ as the door/gate). In the Old Testament context, 'gates' frequently carry the additional meaning of civic power, defense, and the seat of public judgment, a motif carried into the New Testament in phrases like 'the gates of Hades'.\"}",
